    howProcessingWorks: "All tools in this pillar run client-side using JavaScript. Files and file systems is parsed in the browser using native Web APIs, bundled parsers, or WebAssembly modules where appropriate. The input is transformed according to the tool's specific logic — which may include parsing, formatting, validation, conversion, or analysis — and the result is displayed in the output panel. No server round-trip is required for any core utility in this pillar. When Cloud Mode is enabled, AI-powered features (such as natural language understanding, intelligent suggestions, or automated schema generation) may send the user's input to the selected provider with explicit consent. The user must opt in to Cloud Mode before any network request is made. The processing pipeline for each tool follows a consistent architecture: input ingestion and normalization, core transformation logic, and output formatting and rendering. This architecture ensures that deterministic tools always produce the same output for the same input, regardless of network conditions, server availability, or external service status. Batch processing tools apply the same pipeline to each item in the batch sequentially, with progress indicators and the ability to export the full results. The JavaScript engine processes each transformation step in a single-threaded event loop, so users can monitor progress through visual indicators. Error handling is structured so that a failure in one batch item does not halt the entire batch — subsequent items continue to be processed, and a composite error report is generated at the end. This design reflects the philosophy that tools should degrade gracefully and always produce informative, actionable feedback.",
